  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Let\n\n\\[ \\begin{aligned} (1+x+x^2)^n &=a_0+a_1x\\\\ &\\quad+a_2x^2+\\cdots\\\\ &\\quad+a_{2n}x^{2n} \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nbe an identity in \\(x.\\) If we let \\(s=a_0+a_2+a_4+\\cdots+a_{2n},\\) then \\(s\\) equals:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(2^n\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(2^n+1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3^n-1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3^n}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3^n+1}{2}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1966-p36.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(P(x)=(1+x+x^2)^n.\\) Then \\(P(1)=3^n\\) is the sum of all coefficients, while \\(P(-1)=1\\) is the even-coefficient sum minus the odd-coefficient sum. Therefore \\[ s=\\frac{P(1)+P(-1)}2=\\frac{3^n+1}{2}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Evaluate the polynomial at \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=-1\\)",
        "Adding those two values cancels all odd-degree coefficients"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
